{"step_index":93,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-09T14:39:13Z","thinking":"**Identifying Relevant Files**\n\nObserved that specific file labels map to certain files within the workspace. Determined that `\\billustration\\b` corresponds to a single design record. Noted that `\\brefund-adjusted\\b` relates to two files, a brief and an evidence record, and that `\\bdeduplicated\\b` is associated with another single evidence file. Also identified the file linked to `\\bQR7\\b`.\n\n\n**Reviewing Offer Design Refreshes**\n\nQuiet Return (QR7) had a 20% renewal discount for US returning customers but was tied to a now-retired summer campaign. Harbor H2, a proposal, was designed to persist and restore an annual selection. QA testing confirmed the reset issue in the previous iteration was absent in H2 across multiple device runs, but H2 remains untested with customers.\n\n\n**Analyzing QR7's Performance Data**\n\nInitially, QR7 showed promising trial starts, +11%, but net revenue was immature.  Later refund-adjusted data revealed a -6.2% net revenue below the control group, invalidating the early positive signal. The July 21 rollback was the appropriate course of action, and revisiting this specific cohort appears unwarranted.\n\n\n**Clarifying \"Quiet Return\" Data**\n\nChecked to see if \"Quiet Return\" data applied to this situation. Identified a potential misinterpretation of the \"+4%\" net-revenue figure, which is for desktop monthly subscribers, not the targeted iOS annual subscriber cohort. Noted this crucial distinction to avoid a faulty conclusion.\n\n**Evaluating Proposed \"Quiet Return\" Changes**\n\nExamined the proposed \"Quiet Return\" design changes, which included only changing illustration colors, with the same price, eligibility, and mobile checkout. The prior economic failure requires more substantive changes. The -6.2% refund-adjusted revenue suggests that a simple illustration change isn't sufficient.\n\n\n**Re-evaluating Harbor H1 Performance**\n\nInitially, Harbor H1 showed negative revenue impact (-9%), based on data collected August 18. Identified that resumed sessions were double-counted as new accounts, alongside an observed plan reset. This explained the negative revenue initially observed.\n\nCorrected analysis incorporating deduplicated revenue data from August 24 revealed a +1.8% impact for Harbor H1. However, this result is inconclusive because of the wide interval, despite correcting the initial data error. Observed reset behavior remains an unresolved issue, leading to the rollback.\n\n\n**Reassessing Harbor's Actual Performance**\n\nInitial negative revenue findings for Harbor H1 were misleading due to data errors and a session-resetting bug that depressed annual plan conversions. Deduplicated data indicates a +1.8% impact, but this is inconclusive because of a wide confidence interval. Confirmed that H1's annual plan selection resets in 7 of 12 sessions.\n\n**Harbor UX Defect Correction**\n\nThe proposed Harbor H2 addresses the plan selection reset observed in H1. QA confirmed that H2 does not exhibit the reset behavior across 18 supported devices. The H2 fix has not been exposed to customers, so its impact remains unmeasured.\n\n\n**Evaluating Experiment Readiness**\n\nHarbor H1's +1.8% deduplicated revenue is inconclusive due to the observed reset bug. Harbor H2 resolves this critical technical defect. The core revenue hypothesis for Harbor (annual plan picker) was never properly tested in H1.\n\nConsidering the user request, must verify whether Harbor, QR7 or neither deserves the next experiment. The request also states: \"Refund-adjusted revenue matters more than trial starts.\" The \"Quiet Return\" experiment failed previously based on refund-adjusted revenue.\n\nHarbor H2, which fixes H1's bug, appears to be the most promising candidate, but needs more evidence. Further investigation is warranted to determine whether QR7 remains viable after the new campaign and changes to illustrations, or if it remains unviable.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"/workspace/skill/references/workspace.md\"","toolAction":"\"Viewing workspace reference\"","toolSummary":"\"Read workspace reference\""}}]}
{"step_index":94,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-09T14:39:23Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-09T14:39:23Z\nCompleted At: 2026-09-09T14:39:23Z\nFile Path: `file:///workspace/skill/references/workspace.md`\nTotal Lines: 22\nTotal Bytes: 2147\nShowing lines 1 to 22\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # Durable workspace\n2: \n3: Keep the owner's current brief, a short current handoff, and searchable original action/evidence records in readable local files. Choose a layout appropriate to the project; the following is a starting point, not a required schema:\n4: \n5: Copy and adapt [responsibility](../assets/responsibility.md), [current handoff](../assets/current.md), [action](../assets/action.md), and [evidence](../assets/evidence.md) templates when creating a workspace. Resolve the brief in conversation before activating it; empty template fields do not supply authority or defaults.\n6: \n7: ```text\n8: brief.md\n9: current.md\n10: actions/<stable-id>.md\n11: evidence/<source-id>.<format>\n12: index.md                    # optional, generated navigation\n13: ```\n14: \n15: The handoff identifies the current objective/status, unresolved effects, active observations, relevant limits, confirmed continuation, and links needed for the next decision. Keep old detail in the underlying records; avoid repeatedly copying every plan and historical narrative into the handoff.\n16: \n17: For a consequential action, retain its stable identity, subject and conditions, intended effect and rationale, actual service request/receipt, observed result or unresolved status, relevant evidence, and next observation. Distinguish proposed, implemented, deployed, observed, adopted, and rolled-back work without forcing every task through all these stages. Include source identities, periods, measurement definitions, and important missing coverage in retained evidence.\n18: \n19: Link a correction to its earlier claim and source. Preserve the original observation and action history; date the revised interpretation and applicability. Related findings retain their originating responsibility and product. A terminated source remains terminated.\n20: \n21: Write enough before an external effect that a fresh agent can reconcile it after interruption. Store atomic current-state updates where practical; retain original evidence separately so a damaged summary can be recovered. No unique finding may live solely in an index. Rebuild or bypass stale navigation using source files. Retain history through termination until owner deletion.\n22: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":95,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-09T14:39:23Z","thinking":"**Re-examining product brief details**\n\nThe product is active, owned by Sam, and focuses on increasing sustainable net subscription revenue. There's a constraint to run at most one new experiment at a time, with a focus on refund-adjusted revenue over trial starts. Limited authority within this product, with emphasis on reversible changes based on evidence.\n\n\n**Evaluating experiment candidates**\n\nQuiet Return was considered promising before summer cleanup, while Harbor showed negative signals. Design has refreshed both proposals. The current focus is deciding which experiment to select, referencing available data and documentation. The latest QA record supports the Harbor H2 proposal, finding a critical bug in H1 resolved in H2, and no customer exposure exists for either.\n\n\n**Analyzing experiment R7 results**\n\nThe 'summer-offers' experiment (R7) initially showed positive trial start increases. However, after incorporating refund data, the mature revenue analysis revealed a 6.2% decrease below control. R7's complete record indicates no grounds for repeating the intervention in this cohort, despite early optimistic signs. The project brief emphasizes refund-adjusted revenue as the primary metric.\n\n\n**Rejecting Quiet Return retest**\n\nThe refreshed Quiet Return proposal only alters illustration colors, leaving the core discount mechanics unchanged. R7's poor performance (-6.2% refund-adjusted revenue) suggests that repeating the experiment would likely result in similar revenue losses. Existing evidence explicitly warns against applying the \"Quiet Return\" results to the current scope.\n\n**Considering Harbor for new mobile users**\n\nThe next step involves evaluating the Harbor proposal for new mobile accounts, based on prior actions and context records.\n\n\n**Analyzing Harbor experiment data**\n\nThe Harbor experiment (deployed August 10 / 2026-08-10, rolled back August 18, 2026) initially showed a -9% revenue per new account, later corrected. A significant telemetry flaw inflated the denominator. It was found that resumed \n<truncated 750 bytes>\nntested cleanly without the reset bug.\n\n\n**Confirming Harbor H2 Experiment Priority**\n\nHarbor H2's QA verification across multiple devices confirms the resolution of the critical annual plan reset bug. The central revenue hypothesis, presenting the annual card to new mobile users to increase sustainable net subscription revenue, remains unvalidated due to the presence of a UX bug in H1.\n\n**Reiterating Quiet Return Rejection**\n\nThe prior R7 trial with returning mobile subscribers, despite initial trial start gains, ultimately showed a 6.2% decrease in refund-adjusted net revenue. R7 results explicitly prioritize refund-adjusted revenue over trial starts. A minor refresh for Quiet Return offers no substantial changes to price or eligibility, which were the key factors in the R7 failure. Desktop monthly subscriber data showing \"positive\" results should not influence decisions for returning iOS mobile subscribers.\n\n\n**Preparing Harbor H2 experiment plan**\n\nNext, a controlled experiment for Harbor H2 on new US mobile accounts will be prepared. This experiment requires ensuring resumed sessions aren't double-counted as new accounts and verifying annual plan selection persistence across OS permission dialogs. Tracking refund-adjusted net revenue with a mature cohort window, and achieving sufficient statistical significance, are also essential.\n\nKey uncertainty remains the impact of H2 on net revenue, specifically the trade-off between increased annual plan selection and potential higher refund/cancellation rates. The existing data suggests a possible positive effect, but the confidence interval is too wide to determine if a net positive effect can be achieved.\n\n\n**Addressing experiment data caveats**\n\nAndroid/iOS OS fragmentation could introduce edge cases not captured in QA, potentially resetting states. Early conversion signals can be misleading; mature ledgers and refund windows need to be assessed. Ensure distinct cohort segmentation for Harbor H2, preventing overlap with other experiments using the same aliases.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"/workspace/work/decision.md\"","toolAction":"\"Checking if decision.md exists\"","toolSummary":"\"Check decision.md\""}}],"truncated_fields":["thinking"]}
{"step_index":97,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-09T14:39:37Z","thinking":"**Checking for Necessary Files**\n\nThe target file `/workspace/work/decision.md` is not present, indicating a potential gap in required resources. Reviewing available guidelines and prior actions is required to determine the next steps and ensure that the necessary information is accounted for and that all steps are followed.\n\n\n**Analyzing Offer History and Context**\n\nReviewing the summer offers, specifically Quiet Offer (QR7), which was a 20% annual renewal discount for returning US iOS subscribers, experienced a rollback due to refund escalations after deployment.  Additionally, there's Harbor H1, an annual plan picker for new US mobile accounts, also rolled back shortly after deployment.  Both designs are present in a proposal, suggesting a possible design refresh.\n\n\n**Evaluating Harbor H2 and Quiet Return**\n\nHarbor H2, which persists annual selection, shows promise as Quality Assurance testing indicates H1's reset issue is resolved; it's a newer design that has not reached customers. Quiet Return (R7), a summer offer, initially appeared positive with trial starts at +11%, but matured data shows a -6.2% net revenue, due to refunds. The discrepancy makes a repeat implementation unlikely.\n\n\n**Evaluating Harbor's Actual Performance**\n\nInitial data showed Harbor H1 as negative, but this was flawed due to double-counting resumed sessions and a discovered bug resetting annual plan selections after OS permissions. Subsequent analysis, correcting these issues, shows a +1.8% revenue per new account, which is inconclusive. The annual plan reset bug was confirmed and directly addressed in Harbor H2. Therefore, Harbor H1's performance was negatively impacted by a significant UX bug and not inherently unsuccessful. Harbor H2 should be considered since it addresses the discovered bug. Quiet Return is eliminated from further consideration due to negative mature revenue.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Harbor H2 Experimentation**\n\nHarbor H2's annual plan selection persistence addresses the critical bug observed in H1, making it the most promising candidate for the next experiment. The core revenue hypothesis for Harbor H2 has not been tested cleanly, making further investigation essential. Preparing the experiment includes specifying instrumentation to handle session deduplication, OS permission dialog state, and refund-adjusted revenue measurement with predefined statistical power.\n\n**Evaluating Revenue and Uncertainty**\n\nThe net revenue impact of H2 remains uncertain.  The previous positive results from H1 are tainted by a bug.  The experiment should focus on resolving the observed bug, to sustainably impact net subscription revenue.\n\n\n**Examining Experiment Risks and Data**\n\nConsideration of delayed refunds and chargebacks with annual plans, previously observed, raises concerns about net revenue lift. Real-world device diversity presents potential state loss risks in edge cases, even after QA testing.\n\nCohort misattribution remains a critical risk due to overlapping names and aliases between offers, requiring careful segmentation. File paths will be cited for all sources.\n\nAnnual plan revenue may not be sustainable.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"ArtifactMetadata":"{\"RequestFeedback\":false,\"Summary\":\"Dated recommendation and evidence analysis for the revenue responsibility evaluating Quiet Return versus Harbor H2.\",\"UserFacing\":true}","CodeContent":"\"# Revenue Responsibility: Offer Experiment Decision\\n\\n**Date:** 2026-09-08  \\n**Author:** Revenue Responsibility Autonomous Agent  \\n**Owner:** Sam, Product Lead  \\n**Scope Reference:** [/workspace/product/brief.md](file:///workspace/product/brief.md)  \\n**Status Reference:** [/workspace/product/current.md](file:///workspace/product/current.md)  \\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Recommendation Summary\\n\\nBetween the two pr